{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can solve teacher-style series, parallel and mixed circuit problems for current and voltage, with each answer justified by a rule you can state, before the quiz on 2026-10-10."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "My teacher-written circuit quiz is in two days. I need a defensible short plan; explicitly explain anything sacrificed. There are no verified topic weights.",
          "explanation": "Only 25 minutes are available on 2026-10-08 and 25 on 2026-10-09; the quiz is on the excluded exam day 2026-10-10 with no study allowed. That is 50 usable minutes. The four requested topics are tightly nested: current/voltage rules are the prerequisite for series, series and parallel rules are both prerequisites for mixed circuits. Readiness evidence is empty, so I treat this as uncertainty rather than proof of beginner status and embed the necessary rule teaching inside the practice rather than adding separate preparatory lessons. The first 25-minute block builds the current/voltage and series rules and applies them to series problems; the second 25-minute block builds the parallel rule, contrasts it with series, and applies both to mixed circuits. This is the smallest useful sequence that reaches the accepted outcome. Because there are no verified topic weights, I do not claim any topic is more exam-important; the order is educational dependency order only. Sacrificed: mixed-circuit problem volume, timed full-paper simulation, and error-log analysis are not feasible in 50 minutes and are not booked; they are named as deferred scope rather than silently dropped.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 50,
            "highMinutes": 55
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_1",
              "title": "Circuit rules and series application",
              "outcome": "State and apply the current/voltage rules and the series-circuit rules to solve series problems.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 20,
                "likelyMinutes": 25,
                "highMinutes": 30
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You can solve a series circuit for current and voltage and justify each step with a stated rule."
            },
            {
              "localId": "phase_2",
              "title": "Parallel and mixed-circuit application",
              "outcome": "Apply the parallel rule and combine series and parallel rules to solve mixed circuits.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 20,
                "likelyMinutes": 25,
                "highMinutes": 30
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You can solve a mixed circuit for current and voltage, correctly identifying which parts are series and which are parallel."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Current and voltage rules are the conceptual prerequisite for every circuit analysis; taught and applied inside the series and mixed practice activities."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Series-circuit rules are built and applied in the first committed activity, including the shared-current and voltage-sum rules."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Parallel-circuit rules are built and contrasted with series in the second committed activity."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_4",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Mixed circuits are the integration target of the second committed activity, applying both series and parallel rules to one circuit."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "series_rules_practice",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_1"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Build and apply current/voltage and series-circuit rules",
            "activityType": "Guided rule building and problem solving",
            "purpose": "Establish the current/voltage and series rules and immediately apply them to teacher-style series problems, so the first phase is learned and exercised in one block.",
            "instructions": "Work in one 25-minute block. First, write the two rules you need: (1) current is the same at every point in a single series loop; (2) the source voltage equals the sum of the voltages across the series components (V_total = V1 + V2 + ...). If your teacher has given a different notation, use the teacher's notation. Then solve these series problems, showing the rule you used at each step:\n1. A 12 V source is connected to two resistors in series, R1 = 200 ohm and R2 = 400 ohm. Find the current and the voltage across each resistor.\n2. A 9 V source is connected to three resistors in series, R1 = 100 ohm, R2 = 200 ohm, R3 = 300 ohm. Find the current and the voltage across each resistor.\n3. A series circuit has a 6 V source and two resistors. The voltage across R1 is 2 V. Find the voltage across R2 and explain which rule gives it.\nCheck each answer against the rule you stated: current must be the same through every component, and the component voltages must add to the source voltage. If your teacher's notes define current or voltage differently, use the teacher's definition and say so. Do not spend time on parallel or mixed circuits in this block.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written the current/voltage and series rules and solved all three series problems with correct current and voltage values, each justified by a stated rule.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 25,
              "highMinutes": 30
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 25,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "parallel_mixed_practice",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_2"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Build the parallel rule and solve mixed circuits",
            "activityType": "Guided rule building and problem solving",
            "purpose": "Add the parallel rule, contrast it with the series rule, and integrate both in a mixed circuit so the second phase reaches the quiz outcome.",
            "instructions": "Work in one 25-minute block. First, write the parallel rule: in parallel branches the voltage across each branch is the same, and the total current is the sum of the branch currents (I_total = I1 + I2 + ...). Then solve these problems, stating which rule you used:\n1. A 12 V source is connected to two resistors in parallel, R1 = 200 ohm and R2 = 400 ohm. Find the current in each branch and the total current from the source.\n2. Mixed circuit: a 12 V source feeds R1 = 100 ohm in series with a parallel pair R2 = 200 ohm and R3 = 200 ohm. Find the current through R1, the voltage across the parallel pair, and the current in each parallel branch.\n3. Explain in one or two sentences how you decided which parts of problem 2 are series and which are parallel, and why the series rule applies to one part while the parallel rule applies to the other.\nCheck each answer: in the parallel parts the branch voltages must be equal, and the branch currents must add to the current entering the parallel section. If your teacher's notation or rules differ, use the teacher's version and note the difference. Do not add extra problem sets; the goal is one correct mixed-circuit solution with a clear rule-based explanation.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written the parallel rule and solved both problems with correct currents and voltages, and your explanation correctly identifies the series and parallel parts of the mixed circuit.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 25,
              "highMinutes": 30
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 25,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "series_rules_practice"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The quiz covers current, voltage, series, parallel and mixed circuits at the level of the teacher-written problems, using Ohm's law and the series/parallel rules.",
            "The learner has access to the teacher's notation and definitions; where they differ from the standard rules stated here, the teacher's version is used."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "With only 50 usable minutes before the quiz, mixed-circuit problem volume and timed simulation are sacrificed; the plan books one correct mixed-circuit solution with explanation instead of a full problem set.",
            "No verified topic weights exist, so all four requested topics receive work in dependency order rather than weighting by assumed exam importance."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The exact quiz format and whether it includes numerical problems, conceptual questions or both is unknown.",
            "The teacher's notation and any specific rule statements are unknown; the learner is instructed to follow the teacher's version."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when both committed activities are complete and checked, or when the 50 available minutes before the quiz are used, whichever comes first."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "Only 50 minutes are usable before the 2026-10-10 quiz because 2026-10-10 and the following days are excluded and the two available days cap at 25 minutes each. I book two 25-minute committed activities: one for current/voltage and series rules with application, one for the parallel rule and a mixed-circuit integration. This reaches the accepted outcome at the smallest useful scale. Sacrificed: extra mixed-circuit volume, a timed full-paper simulation, and error-log analysis are deferred, not silently dropped. No topic is claimed to be more exam-important because no verified weights were supplied."
}

