{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "A working script that reads a small expenses CSV, prints per-category totals, and flags malformed rows, demonstrated on a supplied sample file."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "ROADMAP",
          "outcome": "Build a small CSV expenses report in Python that groups by category, reports totals, and checks malformed rows.",
          "explanation": "The learner has never read a CSV in Python, so the useful next checkpoint is a first working end-to-end script on a small sample file rather than the whole eventual project. Because the outcome is one small bounded artifact, the necessary constructs (opening a file, using csv.reader, accumulating totals in a dictionary, detecting bad rows) are best introduced inside that single integrated task instead of as separate preparatory lessons. A second activity is included only because the learner's own data file creation is a distinct, genuinely useful step that makes the checkpoint runnable without a nonexistent sample; it is kept small. No further engineering, tooling or project milestones are booked now; they remain strategic and unbooked until this checkpoint works and is independently demonstrated.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 60,
            "likelyMinutes": 100,
            "highMinutes": 160
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_baseline",
              "title": "Working baseline report",
              "outcome": "A runnable Python script that reads a small expenses CSV, reports per-category totals, and flags malformed rows.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 60,
                "likelyMinutes": 100,
                "highMinutes": 160
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "The script runs on the learner's own sample file and produces correct totals plus a list of any malformed rows."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Reading the CSV file is the entry point of the baseline report and is taught inside the integrated build task."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Grouping totals by category is the core computation of the baseline report and is taught inside the integrated build task."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Malformed-row handling is part of the baseline report's correctness and is taught inside the integrated build task."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_4",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The small expenses report is the checkpoint artifact itself; it is built and demonstrated in the integrated task."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "sample_data",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_baseline"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Create a small expenses CSV sample file",
            "activityType": "Data preparation",
            "purpose": "Provides the concrete input the learner will use to build and test the report, since no sample file is supplied.",
            "instructions": "Create a file named expenses.csv in your working folder. Include a header row with the columns date,category,amount. Add at least 8 data rows with a few different categories (for example food, transport, books) and realistic amounts. Deliberately include 2 malformed rows that the report should catch: one row with a non-numeric amount (for example 'abc') and one row with a missing field (for example only two commas-separated values). Keep the file small enough to read by eye. Save it and note which rows you expect the script to flag.",
            "completionCriterion": "expenses.csv exists with the header date,category,amount, at least 8 well-formed rows across multiple categories, and exactly 2 rows you can identify as malformed.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 10,
              "likelyMinutes": 15,
              "highMinutes": 25
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 25,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "build_report",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_baseline"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Build and run the CSV expenses report",
            "activityType": "Guided programming task",
            "purpose": "Teaches the needed constructs and produces the working baseline report in one integrated piece of work.",
            "instructions": "Write a Python script report.py that does the following. 1) Open expenses.csv and read it with the csv module: use csv.reader for a simple approach, or csv.DictReader if you prefer named columns. 2) Skip the header row. 3) For each row, treat it as malformed and record it if it does not have exactly three fields, or if the amount field cannot be converted with float(). 4) For valid rows, add the amount into a dictionary keyed by category. 5) After reading, print each category and its total, and print the list of malformed rows with a short reason. 6) Run the script on your expenses.csv and compare the output with what you expected when you created the file. Fix any mistakes in the script until the totals and the flagged rows match your expectations. Work through the csv module documentation or a short example if the reading step is unfamiliar; the goal is a script you can run and explain, not a memorized API.",
            "completionCriterion": "report.py runs without crashing on expenses.csv, prints a total for every category present, and flags exactly the rows you identified as malformed, with the totals matching the values in the file.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 50,
              "likelyMinutes": 85,
              "highMinutes": 135
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 135,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "sample_data"
            ],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 45,
                "completionCriterion": "The script reads expenses.csv and prints each raw row, confirming the file is being read correctly."
              },
              {
                "afterMinutes": 90,
                "completionCriterion": "The script prints per-category totals for valid rows and identifies the malformed rows with a reason."
              }
            ],
            "stoppingCondition": "Stop once the script runs on your sample file and the totals and flagged rows match your expectations."
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has basic Python syntax (variables, loops, dictionaries) but no prior CSV reading experience.",
            "The learner can create and save a text file in their working environment."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "The integrated build task combines reading, grouping and malformed-row handling instead of separate lessons, because the bounded artifact is small and separating them would add overhead without distinct evidence.",
            "Only one small data-preparation activity is booked; further project milestones (larger files, reporting formats, tooling) are left unbooked until the baseline works."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The learner's exact Python level is unknown; if basic syntax is also new, the build task may need more time than estimated.",
            "The learner's working environment (editor, terminal) is not specified, which may affect setup time."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "The checkpoint is reached when report.py runs on the learner's own sample file and produces correct totals and a correct list of malformed rows."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "This blueprint books only the smallest useful checkpoint: creating a small sample CSV and building one integrated script that reads it, groups totals and flags malformed rows. Separate preparatory lessons were avoided because the artifact is small and the constructs are best learned while building it. The sample-data activity is included because no sample file is supplied and the learner must have concrete input to test against. Later project milestones remain strategic and unbooked until this baseline works and is independently demonstrated, at which point fresh evidence and an explicit request would justify expansion."
}

